.he "cat (1)                      8/16/26                      cat (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
cat
- catenate and print files
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
cat [ -benstuv ] [ file ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Cat reads each file in turn and writes it to the standard output, so
cat file prints one file and cat file1 file2 > file3 concatenates the
first two into the third.  With no file, or where a file is -, it
reads the standard input.
.sp
Output is buffered in 512 byte blocks unless -u makes it unbuffered.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-n	Number the output lines, from 1.

.ti -10
-b	Like -n, but leave blank lines unnumbered.

.ti -10
-s	Crush a run of blank lines into a single blank line.

.ti -10
-v	Make non-printing characters visible.  Control characters print as ^X, the delete character as ^?, and a byte with the high bit set as M- followed by its low seven bits.

.ti -10
-e	Print a $ at the end of each line; implies -v.

.ti -10
-t	Print tab characters as ^I; implies -v.

.ti -10
-u	Write the output unbuffered.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
Beware of cat a b > a, which destroys the input before it is read.
.sp
The Berkeley cat sized its buffer from the file system; micronix has
no such size, so the copy reads 512 bytes, the disk block, at a time.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
cp (1), sh (1)
